# Snap Apps — deploy/.env
#
# Copy to `deploy/.env` (git-ignored — never commit a filled-in copy) and
# fill in real values. This file is read by `deploy/docker-compose.yml` via
# `env_file: .env` (applies to api/worker/web/caddy) and by `deploy/deploy.sh`,
# which sources it directly to drive `packages/db/scripts/db.mjs`.
#
# No default here is a usable secret. Where a variable is missing, the
# compose file's `${VAR:?message}` refuses to start rather than silently
# using an empty string — same posture as apps/server/src/config.ts.
#
# Generate secrets with, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -hex 32          # TOKEN_SECRET, ADMIN_KMS_MASTER_KEY
#   openssl rand -base64 24       # POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD, APP_DB_PASSWORD

# ── Postgres ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Superuser password for the `postgres` role compose creates the container
# with. The API and worker NEVER connect as this role or with this password —
# deploy.sh uses `docker exec ... psql` (local socket, trust auth, exactly
# like packages/db/scripts/db.mjs) to run migrations and to create the two
# real application roles below. Still needs to be a real secret: anyone who
# can reach 5432 with it can log in as postgres.
POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=

# Shared password for the two LOGIN roles deploy.sh creates via
# `db.mjs appuser` — snap_app (NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS, member of app_rw) and
# snap_worker (NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS, member of app_worker). One password
# for both is what db.mjs's appuser command supports; rotating it means
# re-running that command and restarting api+worker.
APP_DB_PASSWORD=

# Container name for the Postgres service. Matches
# docker-compose.yml's `container_name`, and is what deploy.sh passes as
# SNAP_PG_CONTAINER to packages/db/scripts/db.mjs so it execs into the right
# container. Change only if you also change it in docker-compose.yml.
SNAP_PG_CONTAINER=snap-postgres

# ── Server (apps/server/src/config.ts) ──────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE_URL and WORKER_DATABASE_URL are NOT set here — docker-compose.yml
# builds them from APP_DB_PASSWORD above, pointed at the `postgres` service
# on the compose network. Setting them here would be redundant and risks
# drifting from that password.

# 32+ chars, secret, stable. Signs session and upload tokens. ROTATING THIS
# SIGNS EVERYONE OUT — it is not a one-time bootstrap value.
TOKEN_SECRET=

# 64 hex chars. Wraps admin AI-provider keys at rest (apps/server/src/admin/
# crypto/kms.ts). This is the LOCAL KMS stand-in, not a cloud KMS — the
# preflight warns about it and `encryptApiKey` refuses to store or rotate a
# key under it in production (docs/DEPLOY.md §5). Fine to launch with; do not
# treat it as done.
ADMIN_KMS_MASTER_KEY=
# ADMIN_KMS_PROVIDER=local   # default; set to a real KmsProvider's id once one exists

# How this API is reached from OUTSIDE (used to build the Google OAuth
# redirect_uri comparison and in error messages). Must match API_ADDRESS
# below.
PUBLIC_URL=https://api.your-temporary-domain.example

# This site's public web origin. Must match WEB_ADDRESS below and the origin
# actually reachable by users' browsers.
WEB_PUBLIC_URL=https://your-temporary-domain.example

# Comma-separated allow-list of browser origins permitted to call the API.
# NEVER `*` — the preflight refuses to start if it is. Empty refuses every
# cross-origin browser request (fine if only the web app's own server calls
# the API, which is the default shape here).
CORS_ORIGINS=https://your-temporary-domain.example

# ── HOW PEOPLE SIGN IN ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The server REFUSES TO START in production unless at least one of the three
# options below works. That is deliberate: `/v1/auth/sign-in` is 404 in
# production (it was an authentication bypass), so a host with none of these
# configured deploys, looks perfectly healthy, and silently refuses every human
# who visits it. Pick one.
#
# OPTION 1 — Real Google OAuth (docs/DEPLOY.md §3). The production answer.
# Needed on BOTH server and web; only the web app also needs the secret.
# Register ${WEB_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/google/callback as an authorised redirect URI.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# OPTION 2 — Email magic links. Needs a real provider: `auth/mailer.ts` is a
# NoopMailer in production, which logs a warning and sends NOTHING, so an
# unconfigured mailer is worse than none (the UI reports success). Set whichever
# your provider needs.
# MAILER_PROVIDER=
# SMTP_URL=

# OPTION 3 — The Google sign-in SIMULATOR. FOR A DEMO HOST ONLY.
#
# Trades proof of identity for a fixed, server-side allow-list of demo
# accounts. It exists so an investor demo can run before real Google
# credentials are issued, and everything after the identity assertion —
# session cookie, onboarding, workspace selection, RBAC — is the real code.
#
# Triple-gated, and all three are required: NODE_ENV must not be production OR
# DEMO_ENV must be exactly `staging`; GOOGLE_SIGNIN_SIMULATOR must be exactly
# `true`; and real Google credentials must NOT be set (the genuine flow always
# wins). A real production host never sets DEMO_ENV, so it cannot reach this
# path even by accident.
#
# DELETE BOTH LINES the moment real credentials exist.
# DEMO_ENV=staging
# GOOGLE_SIGNIN_SIMULATOR=true

# ── CREDITS / STRIPE (apps/server/src/credits/) ──────────────────────────────
#
# Three states, decided in apps/server/src/integrations/simulation.ts, real
# credentials ALWAYS win:
#   absent    — no credentials and no simulator flag (the DEFAULT). The buy
#               screen truthfully says card payments are not connected; nothing
#               is charged and nothing is granted. Correct for today.
#   simulated — STRIPE_SIMULATOR=true AND not production (or DEMO_ENV=staging).
#               Speaks Stripe's Checkout REST shape and Stripe-Signature HMAC.
#               Fatal at boot if asked for on a production host.
#   real      — STRIPE_SECRET_KEY set. The webhook becomes the ONLY path that
#               turns money into credits (stripe-webhook.controller.ts).
#
# REFUSED AT SELECTION: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY set but STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET not —
# without the signing secret, a completed payment is taken and never
# fulfilled, and the failure is invisible until a customer complains.
#STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
#STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#STRIPE_SIMULATOR=

# Where original captures persist on the HOST. Bind-mounted straight into
# the api and worker at /data/storage (see docker-compose.yml) — legal records under ATO
# rules, must survive container/volume recreation and be reachable by
# rsync/restic, never live only inside a container layer.
STORAGE_HOST_DIR=/opt/snap-apps/data/storage

# Which extraction provider the worker uses.
EXTRACTION_PROVIDER=ollama
EXTRACTION_MODEL=gemma4:31b

# Path to a KEY=VALUE file on the HOST holding the Ollama Cloud API key (see
# ~/.claude/secrets/ollama-cloud.env for this project's shared dev key —
# provision a SEPARATE key for production, do not reuse the shared/rate-
# limited dev one). Bind-mounted read-only into the worker container; never
# put the key itself in this file, in the image, or in compose. chmod 600,
# owned by the app user, outside the repo.
OLLAMA_ENV_FILE_HOST=/etc/snap-apps/secrets/ollama.env

# ── SNAPPER, THE WEBSITE ASSISTANT (docs/ASSISTANT.md §8) ────────────────────
#
# The model key is NOT set here. The api reads the OPENROUTER_API_KEY= line of
# the same OLLAMA_ENV_FILE_HOST file above (now mounted into the api as well as
# the worker). No such line = the assistant reports ok:false and stays hidden.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION, on api AND web (compose passes it to both). The web
# container's same-origin proxy sends it as X-Assistant-Proxy; the api answers
# 404 to anything without it, so the public API host cannot be used to skip
# the origin check or spoof the client IP. Unset in production = assistant
# disabled (fail closed); the API itself still boots. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
ASSISTANT_PROXY_SECRET=
# OpenRouter model id, and an optional fallback used ONLY for the single retry
# before the first token. Defaults: z-ai/glm-5.3-flash, no fallback.
#ASSISTANT_MODEL=z-ai/glm-5.3-flash
#ASSISTANT_FALLBACK_MODEL=
# Answers per UTC day across all visitors before the assistant says it is
# busy. Per-IP limits (6/min, 40/h, 150/day) apply regardless.
#ASSISTANT_DAILY_LIMIT=3000
# 1 = the question and the answer text go into the api's JSON log line (emails
# and long numbers redacted). Default 0: no message text is logged at all.
#ASSISTANT_LOG_TRANSCRIPTS=0

# The lowest Android build (versionCode) still allowed to run. An app below it
# is blocked by a full-screen "update required" until it installs the published
# build, so only raise it to a number the published build already meets (the
# manifest at /v1/downloads/android/manifest shows its versionCode). 0 = no
# minimum, the default. Must be a whole number or the API refuses to boot.
#ANDROID_MIN_VERSION_CODE=0

NODE_ENV=production

# ── YourTal (apps/server/src/config.ts) ─────────────────────────────────────
# YourTal is the sister app that holds the point wallet and voucher store.
# Staging is https://yourtal.gaiada.com. Both URLs are ORIGINS with no path:
# YourTal serves its web app at the root, its API under /api/ (partner calls
# are POST /api/partners/actions) and its voucher service under /v1/vouchers/,
# all on that one host, so YOURTAL_API_URL does not carry an /api suffix.
YOURTAL_WEB_URL=https://yourtal.gaiada.com
YOURTAL_API_URL=https://yourtal.gaiada.com

# The partner link is OFF until YourTal issues Snap Apps a partner id and
# secret. Both are optional and the server boots without them. The id is not
# secret. The SECRET never goes in this file: it lives in its own host file,
# /etc/snap-apps/secrets/yourtal.env (one YOURTAL_PARTNER_SECRET=... line,
# chmod 600, readable by uid 1001), mounted read-only into the api (link and
# unlink) and the worker (the receipt-report outbox) and located there by
# YOURTAL_ENV_FILE, which compose sets. Setting YOURTAL_PARTNER_SECRET in the
# environment also works, for local runs only.
#
# Order matters: copy the secret file onto the host FIRST, then set
# YOURTAL_ENV_FILE_HOST. Left unset, compose mounts /dev/null and the link is
# off. Set to a file that is not there, deploy.sh warns and mounts /dev/null
# for that run rather than letting Docker create a directory at the path (see
# the note on the mount in docker-compose.yml).
# YOURTAL_ENV_FILE_HOST=/etc/snap-apps/secrets/yourtal.env
# YOURTAL_PARTNER_ID=snap-app
#
# The receipt content-ref key lives in that SAME host file, as a second line,
# YOURTAL_RECEIPT_REF_KEY=... (at least 32 characters). It makes a re-photo of
# one paper receipt, in any workspace and by any person, report under the same
# ref so YourTal pays it once. Generate it on the box, once:
#   printf 'YOURTAL_RECEIPT_REF_KEY=%s
' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> /etc/snap-apps/secrets/yourtal.env
# It is Snap Apps' own: never copy it anywhere, never send it to YourTal, never
# put it in this file. NEVER ROTATE IT: a new key gives every receipt already
# reported a new ref, and they could all be paid again. Unset (or under 32
# characters) the server still runs, reports receipts under their image hash,
# and logs one warning that cross-account duplicate protection is off. The api
# and the worker both mount the file.
#
# The voucher (merchant API) credential is two more lines in that SAME host
# file, issued by YourTal for the Snap Apps merchant:
#   YOURTAL_MERCHANT_KEY_ID=mc_<uuid>
#   YOURTAL_MERCHANT_SECRET=<64 hex characters = 32 raw bytes>
# Both are read from the environment first, else from the file by name. A
# missing or malformed value counts as unset (one warning, never the value),
# and the voucher feature stays off: it needs the partner link above AND both.

# ── Sign in with YourTal (group task 3.3) ───────────────────────────────────
# SnapApp as an OpenID Connect client of YourTal
# (yourtal/docs/26-sign-in-with-yourtal.md). OFF while YOURTAL_OIDC_ISSUER is
# unset; each client is on once its id is set too. https only in production.
#YOURTAL_OIDC_ISSUER=https://yourtal.gaiada.com/oidc
#YOURTAL_OIDC_WEB_CLIENT_ID=snapapp-staging-web
#YOURTAL_OIDC_MOBILE_CLIENT_ID=snapapp-staging-mobile
#
# Redirect URIs must match YourTal's registration character for character.
# Defaults: ${WEB_PUBLIC_URL}/auth/yourtal/callback for the website (staging:
# https://snap-apps.gaiada.com/auth/yourtal/callback) and
# au.com.snapapps.app:/oauth/yourtal for the phone app.
#YOURTAL_OIDC_WEB_REDIRECT_URI=https://snap-apps.gaiada.com/auth/yourtal/callback
#YOURTAL_OIDC_MOBILE_REDIRECT_URI=au.com.snapapps.app:/oauth/yourtal
#
# The website client authenticates with private_key_jwt. Its PRIVATE key never
# goes in this file: it is its own host file, mounted read-only into the api.
# Make it once, on a trusted machine, with
#   pnpm --filter @snap/server yourtal:oidc-key new ./yourtal-oidc-web.jwk
# which writes the private JWK (mode 0600) and prints the PUBLIC JWK to send
# to YourTal for snapapp-staging-web. Copy the private file to
# /etc/snap-apps/secrets/yourtal-oidc-web.jwk (chmod 600, chown 1001), delete
# the local copy, THEN set:
#YOURTAL_OIDC_KEY_FILE_HOST=/etc/snap-apps/secrets/yourtal-oidc-web.jwk
# Unset, compose mounts /dev/null and the website client stays off (the phone
# client does not need a key). Named but missing, deploy.sh warns and does
# the same.

# ── Reverse proxy / TLS (deploy/Caddyfile) ──────────────────────────────────
# Must include the scheme. A bare https://host gets Caddy's automatic
# Let's Encrypt; deploy/.env.local.example uses http://localhost:PORT
# instead, which disables TLS for local verification (no public domain to
# issue a cert for, and port 80/443 may not even be reachable from outside).
API_ADDRESS=https://api.your-temporary-domain.example
WEB_ADDRESS=https://your-temporary-domain.example
# Required by Let's Encrypt for expiry notices; any address you read.
CADDY_ACME_EMAIL=ops@your-temporary-domain.example
HTTP_PORT=80
HTTPS_PORT=443

# ── Image tag ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# deploy/deploy.sh tags built images with the git short SHA by default and
# writes it here after a successful deploy — keeping the previous value
# around (in `docker images`) is what makes deploy/deploy.sh's rollback path
# work. Leave as `local` for a manual/dev build.
IMAGE_TAG=local

# ── Prebuilt images (recommended on a small VPS) ─────────────────────────────
#
# Set these and run `deploy/deploy.sh --pull` to pull images built by
# .github/workflows/publish-images.yml instead of compiling on the box.
#
# Why it matters here: `next build` is the most memory-hungry step in this
# stack and is what gets OOM-killed on a 2 GB VPS, failing with an error that
# reads like a code fault rather than a capacity one. Pulling also means the
# artifact CI tested is the artifact that runs, rather than two builds that
# merely share a commit.
#
# The repository is private, so its packages are too: the host needs a one-off
#   docker login ghcr.io -u <github-user> -p <token-with-read:packages>
#
# IMAGE_TAG must name an EXACT build (the workflow tags `sha-<short>`), never
# `latest` — a rollback has to be able to name what it is rolling back to.
# SERVER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/gaiadabali/snap-server
# WEB_IMAGE=ghcr.io/gaiadabali/snap-web
# DOCAI_IMAGE is OPTIONAL: deploy.sh derives it from SERVER_IMAGE's own
# registry and owner when unset, so an existing host needs no edit to pick
# up the sidecar. Set it only to override.
# DOCAI_IMAGE=ghcr.io/gaiadabali/snap-docai
# IMAGE_TAG=sha-1a2b3c4

# ── The OCR sidecar (services/docai-engine) ─────────────────────────────────
#
# Internal to the compose network; never published to the host. The WORKER
# reaches it at http://docai:8088 and that default needs no configuration —
# these exist to change the model tier or to point at a sidecar running
# elsewhere.
#
# `mobile` is ~5.7s per receipt on CPU; `server` is ~31s for better accuracy.
# The build arg and the runtime value must MATCH, or the container downloads a
# second model set on first use and the build-time bake was wasted.
# DOCAI_MODEL_TIER=mobile
# DOCAI_DEVICE=cpu
#
# Leave DOCAI_ENABLE_MKLDNN false. paddlepaddle 3.3.1's PIR executor throws
# ConvertPirAttribute2RuntimeAttribute on both PP-OCRv5 detection models with
# oneDNN on — verified and reproducible. docs/GAPS.md A4 evaluates OpenVINO as
# the alternative CPU accelerator.
# DOCAI_ENABLE_MKLDNN=false
#
# Heuristic operating point, NOT calibrated (D20 / docs/GAPS.md B3).
# DOCAI_MIN_CONFIDENCE=0.3
#
# Set this to disable the OCR shadow stage entirely (unset = no sidecar call at
# all, which is what shipped until 2026-09-16). Normally leave it alone: the
# compose default points the worker at the docai service.
# DOCAI_SIDECAR_URL=http://docai:8088
